# What's New in EF Core 10

EF Core 10.0 (EF10) was released in November 2025 and is a Long Term Support (LTS) release. EF10 will be supported until November 10, 2028.

Tip

You can run and debug into the samples by [downloading the sample code from GitHub](https://github.com/dotnet/EntityFramework.Docs). Each section below links to the source code specific to that section.

EF10 requires the .NET 10 SDK to build and requires the .NET 10 runtime to run. EF10 will not run on earlier .NET versions, and will not run on .NET Framework.

The below release notes list the major improvements in the release; [the full list of issues for the release can be found here](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues?q=is%3Aissue%20milestone%3A10.0.0).

## Azure SQL and SQL Server

### Vector search support

EF 10 brings full support for the recently-introduced [vector data type](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/vector-data-type) and its supporting [`VECTOR_DISTANCE()`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-distance-transact-sql) function, available on Azure SQL Database and on SQL Server 2025. The vector data type allows storing *embeddings*, which are representation of meaning that can be efficiently searched over for similarity, powering AI workloads such as semantic search and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).

To use the `vector` data type, simply add a .NET property of type `SqlVector<float>` to your entity type:

```text
public class Blog
{
    // ...

    [Column(TypeName = "vector(1536)")]
    public SqlVector<float> Embedding { get; set; }
}
```

Then, insert embedding data by populating the Embedding property and calling [SaveChangesAsync](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.dbcontext.savechangesasync) as usual:

```text
IEmbeddingGenerator<string, Embedding<float>> embeddingGenerator = /* Set up your preferred embedding generator */;

var embedding = await embeddingGenerator.GenerateVectorAsync("Some text to be vectorized");
context.Blogs.Add(new Blog
{
    Name = "Some blog",
    Embedding = new SqlVector<float>(embedding)
});
await context.SaveChangesAsync();
```

Finally, use the [VectorDistance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.sqlserverdbfunctionsextensions.vectordistance) [function](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-distance-transact-sql) in your LINQ queries to perform similarity search for a given user query:

```text
var sqlVector = /* some user query which we should search for similarity */;
var topSimilarBlogs = context.Blogs
    .OrderBy(b => EF.Functions.VectorDistance("cosine", b.Embedding, sqlVector))
    .Take(3)
    .ToListAsync();
```

For more information on vector search, [see the documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/providers/sql-server/vector-search).

### JSON type support

EF 10 also fully supports the new [json data type](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/json-data-type), also available on Azure SQL Database and on SQL Server 2025. While SQL Server has included JSON functionality for several versions, the data itself was stored in plain textual columns in the database; the new data type provides significant efficiency improvements and a safer way to store and interact with JSON.

With EF 10, if you've configured EF with [UseAzureSql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.sqlserverdbcontextoptionsextensions.useazuresql) or with a compatibility level of 170 or higher (SQL Server 2025), EF automatically defaults to using the new JSON data type. For example, the following entity type has a primitive collection (Tags, an array of strings) and Details (mapped as a complex type):

```text
public class Blog
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    public string[] Tags { get; set; }
    public required BlogDetails Details { get; set; }
}

public class BlogDetails
{
    public string? Description { get; set; }
    public int Viewers { get; set; }
}

protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
{
    modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>().ComplexProperty(b => b.Details, b => b.ToJson());
}
```

EF 10 creates the following table for the above:

```sql
CREATE TABLE [Blogs] (
    [Id] int NOT NULL IDENTITY,
    [Name] nvarchar(max) NOT NULL,
    [Tags] json NOT NULL,
    [Details] json NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT [PK_Blogs] PRIMARY KEY ([Id])
);
```

LINQ querying is fully supported as well. For example, the following filters for blogs with more than 3 viewers:

```text
var highlyViewedBlogs = await context.Blogs.Where(b => b.Details.Viewers > 3).ToListAsync();
```

This produces the following SQL, making use of the new `JSON_VALUE()` `RETURNING` clause:

```sql
SELECT [b].[Id], [b].[Name], [b].[Tags], [b].[Details]
FROM [Blogs] AS [b]
WHERE JSON_VALUE([b].[Details], '$.Viewers' RETURNING int) > 3
```

Note that if your EF application already uses JSON via `nvarchar` columns, these columns will be automatically changed to `json` with the first migration. You can opt out of this by manually setting the column type to `nvarchar(max)`, or configuring a compatibility level lower than 170.

### Custom default constraint names

EF 10 now allows you to specify a name for default constraints using [HasDefaultValueSql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.relationalpropertybuilderextensions.hasdefaultvaluesql), rather than letting the database generate them:

```text
protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
{
    modelBuilder.Entity<Post>()
        .Property(p => b.CreatedDate)
        .HasDefaultValueSql("GETDATE()", "DF_Post_CreatedDate");
}
```

You can also enable automatic naming by EF of all default constraints using [UseNamedDefaultConstraints](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.sqlservermodelbuilderextensions.usenameddefaultconstraints):

```text
protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
{
    modelBuilder.UseNamedDefaultConstraints();
}
```

Note

If you have existing migrations, the next migration you add will rename every single default constraint in your model.

## Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL

### Full-text search support

Azure Cosmos DB now offers support for [full-text search](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/gen-ai/full-text-search). It enables efficient and effective text searches, as well as evaluating the relevance of documents to a given search query. It can be used in combination with vector search to improve the accuracy of responses in some AI scenarios. EF Core 10 is adding support for this feature allowing for modeling the database with full-text search enabled properties and using full-text search functions inside queries targeting Azure Cosmos DB.

Here is a basic EF model configuration enabling full-text search on one of the properties:

```text
public class Blog
{
    ...

    public string Contents { get; set; }
}

public class BloggingContext
{
    ...

    protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>(b =>
        {
            b.Property(x => x.Contents).EnableFullTextSearch();
            b.HasIndex(x => x.Contents).IsFullTextIndex();
        });
    }
}
```

Once the model is configured, we can use full-text search operations in queries using methods provided in [Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.ef.functions):

```text
var cosmosBlogs = await context.Blogs.Where(x => EF.Functions.FullTextContains(x.Contents, "cosmos")).ToListAsync();
```

The following full-text operations are currently supported: [FullTextContains](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.cosmosdbfunctionsextensions.fulltextcontains), [FullTextContainsAll](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.cosmosdbfunctionsextensions.fulltextcontainsall), [FullTextContainsAny](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.cosmosdbfunctionsextensions.fulltextcontainsany), [FullTextScore](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.cosmosdbfunctionsextensions.fulltextscore). See the Azure Cosmos DB documentation for details: [`FullTextContains`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/nosql/query/fulltextcontains), [`FullTextContainsAll`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/nosql/query/fulltextcontainsall), [`FullTextContainsAny`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/nosql/query/fulltextcontainsany), [`FullTextScore`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/nosql/query/fulltextscore).

For more information on Cosmos full-text search, see the [documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/providers/cosmos/full-text-search).

### Hybrid search

EF Core now supports [Rrf](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.cosmosdbfunctionsextensions.rrf) ([Reciprocal Rank Fusion](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/nosql/query/rrf)) function, which combines vector similarity search and full-text search (i.e. hybr

import { UserRepository, PasswordHasher } from "../ports/UserRepository";

interface RegisterUserInput {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;
}

export class RegisterUser {
  constructor(
    private readonly users: UserRepository,
    private readonly hasher: PasswordHasher,
  ) {}

  async execute(input: RegisterUserInput): Promise<User> {
    const existing = await this.users.findByEmail(input.email);
    if (existing) {
      throw new Error("A user with that email already exists");
    }

    const passwordHash = await this.hasher.hash(input.password);
    const user = User.create(input.id, input.email, passwordHash);

    await this.users.save(user);
    return user;
  }
}TypeScript
```

### The adapter (infrastructure implementation)

The adapter lives in the outer rings and implements the port using a real technology. Here it is an in-memory store for brevity, but a SQL or Prisma implementation would sit in exactly the same place. The arrow of dependency points inward: this file imports the interface, the interface does not import this file.

```text
// infrastructure/InMemoryUserRepository.ts
// Outer layer adapter. Implements the inner-layer port.

import { User } from "../domain/entities/User";
import { UserRepository } from "../application/ports/UserRepository";

export class InMemoryUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  private readonly store = new Map<string, User>();

  async findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> {
    for (const user of this.store.values()) {
      if (user.email === email) return user;
    }
    return null;
  }

  async save(user: User): Promise<void> {
    this.store.set(user.id, user);
  }
}

// composition root (e.g. main.ts) wires concrete classes to the use case:
//   const useCase = new RegisterUser(
//     new InMemoryUserRepository(),
//     new BcryptHasher(),
//   );TypeScript
```

That last comment block, the composition root, is where dependency injection happens. It is the only place that knows about both the use case and the concrete adapters. To switch from an in-memory store to PostgreSQL, you write a `PostgresUserRepository` and change one line in the composition root. The entity, the port, and the use case never change. That is the payoff of the Dependency Rule made concrete.

## Clean vs onion vs hexagonal architecture

This is the comparison people search for most, and the honest answer is that these three patterns are siblings, not rivals. All three insist that source code dependencies point inward and that business logic stays isolated from infrastructure. The differences are mostly emphasis and vocabulary. The table below summarizes how they line up.

| Aspect | Clean architecture | Onion architecture | Hexagonal (Ports &amp; Adapters) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Origin | Robert C. Martin, 2012 | Jeffrey Palermo, 2008 | Alistair Cockburn, 2005 |
| Core idea | Organize around use cases; unify earlier patterns under one Dependency Rule | Protect a central domain model with rings; infrastructure on the outside | Isolate the app core behind ports so any external tech is swappable |
| Layers / terminology | Entities, use cases, interface adapters, frameworks &amp; drivers | Domain model, domain services, application services, infrastructure | Application core surrounded by ports (interfaces) and adapters |
| Shape | Concentric circles | Concentric circles (onion rings) | Hexagon with ports on each edge |
| Dependency direction | Inward, toward entities | Inward, toward the domain model | Inward, toward the application core |
| Distinctive emphasis | Explicit use-case ring; broadest synthesis | Strongest focus on the domain model | Strongest focus on swappable external systems |
| Shared by all three | Inward-only dependencies, dependency inversion via interfaces, business logic isolated from frameworks, databases, and UI |  |  |

The practical conclusion: do not agonize over which name to pick. If you apply the Dependency Rule and keep your business logic free of infrastructure, you have effectively built all three at once. Clean architecture is best understood as the umbrella term that absorbs onion and hexagonal, adding an explicit use-case layer on top.

## Benefits and honest tradeoffs

Clean architecture is genuinely valuable, but it is oversold often enough that an honest accounting matters. Here are the real benefits, followed by the real costs.

### The benefits

- **Testability.** Use cases and entities can be unit tested with fakes, with no database, HTTP server, or framework involved.
- **Independence from frameworks.** The web framework becomes a delivery detail, so upgrades and replacements touch only the outer ring.
- **Deferred decisions.** You can build and test business logic before choosing a database, and swap it later behind a port.
- **Clear boundaries.** New developers can find business rules in one place instead of buried inside controllers and ORM models.

### The tradeoffs and criticisms

- **More code and indirection.** Ports, adapters, and DTO mapping mean more files for each feature, which slows simple changes.
- **Interfaces with one implementation.** Defining a port you only ever implement once can feel like ceremony, and sometimes it is.
- **Dogmatic application.** Teams that treat the four rings as a rigid template, rather than principles, create boilerplate without benefit.
- **Mapping overhead.** Converting between entity, persistence, and transport shapes adds work that thin CRUD apps rarely repay.

## When is clean architecture worth it?

The decision comes down to how complex and how long-lived your business logic is. Reach for clean architecture when business rules are genuinely complex and central to the product, when the codebase will be maintained by a team for years, when you must swap or defer infrastructure choices, or when you need fast, database-free tests for critical logic. In those situations the indirection pays for itself many times over.

Skip it, or apply only a light version, for prototypes, short-lived scripts, simple CRUD applications, and small solo projects where most endpoints just forward data to a database. A pragmatic path many teams take is to start with a simpler layered structure and refactor toward clean architecture exactly when the pain of coupling shows up, for instance when a database change forces edits across dozens of unrelated files. Clean architecture is a destination you can move toward incrementally, not a gate you must pass before writing your first feature.

## Conclusion

Clean architecture is, in the end, one rule wearing several costumes. Keep your source code dependencies pointing inward, define interfaces in the inner layers and implement them on the outside, and let dependency injection wire the two together. Do that and your entities and use cases become independent of frameworks, databases, and UIs, which is the entire point. The four rings, the diagrams, and the comparisons to onion and hexagonal architecture are all just ways of describing that same inward-pointing discipline.

Treat it as a set of principles you apply where coupling actually hurts, not as a template to stamp onto every feature. Start with the concrete TypeScript slice above, feel where the boundaries help, and let your real maintenance pain, not dogma, decide how many layers you need.

